{
	title: 'State paths and safe diagnostics',
	spec: 'Everything Hal writes lives under its home, which is the repo root: git clone is the only install. hal --home <dir> (any command) picks another home; tests set paths.state.home to a temp home, so they never touch live data. The home has two gitignored directories, both persistent: sessions/ (conversations, one directory per session) and state/ (everything else, including socket, host lock and later peer and access keys, so it is created with owner-only permissions). The copied ./auth.ason is input. Diagnostics never print tokens or appear in the model transcript.',
	status: 'done',
	notes: [
		'The artifacts predate the task wording: they use HAL_DIR, HAL_STATE_DIR, an ipc/ dir and paths captured as constants at import. The task wants one HAL_HOME, sessions/ + state/, and README wants config read at call time (tests flip HAL_HOME per test). Take only the chmod-not-mkdir-mode idea (mode is masked by umask; chmod also tightens an old loose dir) and formatHomePath.',
		'Under NODE_ENV=test with no paths.state.home, the home falls back to /tmp/hal-test-home-<pid>. It is a safety net, not cleanup: tests that spawn ./run without --home leave such dirs behind. Pass --home with a temp dir you delete.',
		'Redaction: a key pattern containing "token" also hits usage fields like input_tokens; it matches "token" but not "tokens" on purpose.',
		'diag.log assumes paths.init() has run (state/ exists); it throws otherwise.',
		"Shared main.ts: another agent's commit swept up my uncommitted lines, and my index-built commit then removed theirs because HEAD had moved. Build your main.ts index entry from the current HEAD right before committing, and check the commit's diffstat for unexpected removals.",
		'HAL_HOME became hal --home <dir> (14 Oct, user request: options, never environment variables). In-process tests set paths.state.home; bun -e children set it first in their script (static imports still run before it, so modules must not read the home at import); scripts/cost and change-cleanup.ts take --home. A model running hal inside a --home Hal no longer inherits that home.'
